#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091933 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c7ab50-5a8b-5bcd-8483-cec8bf6bd3ce" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Tangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "5d895685-fc8e-57ca-9552-1d3d2e0202a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3513" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $\\tan x=4$ for $0 \\leq x < 360^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan (4) = 75.96375...^{∘} \\approx 75.964^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $75.964^\\circ$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = 75.964^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx_2 = 180^\\circ + 75.964^\\circ \\approx 255.964^\\circ\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are\n\n$$\nx=76^\\circ, 256^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091934 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f388646-b956-518f-83df-6a49a642bd64" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Tangent with Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "8efb6790-c3c0-5639-bb5d-61b774e1fa60" :question/math-academy-id "e-3514" :question/problem "Find the solutions to the equation $3\\tan x + 3\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\tan x + 3\\sqrt{3} &= 0 \\\\\n3\\tan x &= -3\\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\tan x &= -\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\\n\\tan x &= -\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan (-\\sqrt{3}) =-60^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-60^\\circ$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution. However, to generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x + 180^{∘} =-60^{∘} + 180^{∘} = 120^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x + 360^{∘} =-60^{∘} + 360^{∘} = 300^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=120^\\circ, 300^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091935 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebf8592e-1a43-5d65-af3c-9becd79ae8b9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Tangent Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "d50d2e80-594a-5512-8ebb-f82082def313" :question/math-academy-id "e-3515" :question/problem "Solve the equation $3 \\tan x - \\sqrt{3} = 0$ for $-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\tan x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\tan x - \\sqrt{3} &= 0 \\\\\n3\\tan x &= \\sqrt{3} \\\\\n\\tan x &= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) = 30^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $30^\\circ$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 =30^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} - 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 30^{∘} - 180^{∘} \\\\\n&=-150^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are\n\n$$\nx=-150^\\circ, 30^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091936 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a71da663-e7a4-5781-973c-5dc7e4deb83f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points of Intersection of a Horizontal Line and a Tangent Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "3311aab4-1938-543c-a84c-d285a905dada" :question/math-academy-id "e-3516" :question/problem "The graph below shows a section of the curve $y=\\tan{x}$ on the interval $-\\dfrac \\pi 2 < x < \\dfrac {\\pi} 2$ and the line $y=1$. The line and the curve intersect at the point $A$, as shown. What is the $x$-coordinate of the point $A$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/915/Source/Images/75d3912698b1187b3fc86e2e9db62892.png)" :question/worked-solution "The $y$-values of both functions are the same at the intersection point. Equating the two functions gives\n\n$$\n\\tan x=1, \\qquad -\\dfrac \\pi 2 < x < \\dfrac {\\pi} 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan (1) = \\frac{π}{4}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nlies inside the required domain, and there is only one point of intersection, we conclude that the $x$-coordinate of the intersection point $A$ is\n\n$$\nx= \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$"}]}
